## Pass

- For every supported harness, each part of the statement is stated (not
  implied) by that harness's doc, or shown by one of its cited runs, or listed
  in the cell's `deviations` as a part the harness does not do (cited runs: a <run> is a directory in the project: `setup/` is the
  scenario, each `samples/<ts>/` holds what the real harness did: payloads,
  stream, transcript). A recording outranks a doc: where a run shows the real
  harness doing (or not doing) a part and a doc sentence says otherwise, the
  run decides, and the doc's sentence is not held against the cell.
- **A conflict between a cited doc and a cited recording is disclosed.** For
  every <doc> and <run> of one harness, where the doc says the harness does
  (or does not do) a part and the recording shows the opposite, the cell's
  `deviations` has an entry (adr `capability-grounding`) that starts "Doc and
  recording conflict:", names the doc section, quotes the sentence, and says
  what the recording shows. A conflict nobody disclosed is a failure, even
  though the recording decides it.
- **A deviation never negates what defines the cell.** A supported cell's
  `deviations` leave the statement's defining clause (the part without which
  the harness would not be said to provide the capability) true: if a
  deviation says the harness does NOT do that clause, the cell is not
  supported, and must be `supported: false` with the evidence for the absence.
  Fail a supported cell whose deviation negates its defining clause, and
  name the clause and the deviation.
- The statement claims no part that a providing harness neither documents, shows in a cited run, nor lists as a deviation
  (a part its doc leaves out may be grounded by a run); what
  differs between harnesses (a default, a name) is left out of the statement.
- For every <absent> harness, the claim "this harness lacks the behaviour"
  is shown by its evidence, both kinds counting: (a) its <run kind="absent">
  recordings: the run's `setup/` scenario really attempts the behaviour (read
  it: a scenario that never tries shows nothing), and the samples (payloads,
  stream, transcript) show the real harness not doing the part of the statement
  the reason names, even where a doc describes the behaviour; and/or (b) its
  <doc kind="absent"> pages: read each in full, search it for every part of the
  statement and its synonyms (the event, flag, command or setting that would
  carry it), and pass if the page says the behaviour is unsupported, or
  documents this whole area (its complete list of events, handlers, commands or
  options) and the behaviour is not in it. A harness whose recordings show
  only some parts of the statement is not absent: that cell belongs as
  supported, with the missing parts as its `deviations`, so fail an absent
  claim the recordings partly contradict. The `reason` must say what the
  evidence shows, no more.
- If the capability is newly added: a citation asks to mock this behaviour.

## How to decide

For each subject, make the checklist: every part of the statement (each
value, condition, variant it names), one item per part and per providing
harness; for a removed capability, the item is the citation asking to stop
mocking it. Then find, for each item, the doc line that states it, else the
run file and line that shows it while the doc is silent (or "none").

Your reasoning, pass or fail, is that checklist, one line per item: the
item, the file and line that satisfies it (or "none"). A pass without the
checklist is not a pass.

## Fail

For an <absent> harness: fail if its recordings do not show the absence (a
scenario that never attempts the behaviour, or samples in which the real
harness does it) and its docs do not show it either; if a doc documents the
behaviour and no cited recording shows the harness not doing it; if the
evidence does not cover the area at all (a page that is merely silent on an
unrelated topic is not evidence of absence); or if the reason claims more than
the evidence shows. Quote the line or sample that documents or shows the
behaviour, or say which part of the area the evidence never covers. A <pending> harness is not a failure and not support: never count
it toward the statement.

Name the harness, the part of the statement neither its doc nor its runs
support (or that its doc contradicts, or the empty section: a ref that
resolved to nothing), and quote the doc line closest to it.
